// @run
// @log 3
// @log 7
// @warning This expression has no effect: it is not the final statement of its block, so its value is discarded

// D1645 — a nameless `FuncDecl` is an arrow lambda, an expression, not a declaration: it
// binds no name, so several of them in one module are several discarded values and not a
// redeclaration of one blank name. The rule is the same one a bare `55` statement follows.
(a: i32) => a
(b: i32): i32 => b
() => {
  print(1)
}

// The control the rule is read off: a NAMED declaration at the same position still binds,
// is still reachable by name, and a second `g` would still be `redeclared g`.
function g() { return 3 }
print(g())

// A final bare lambda is the block's value, so it draws no `unused-pure-expression` — the
// same exemption `55` gets as a tail.
print(7)
(c: i32) => c
